{title: The Old Rugged Cross}
{lyricist: George Bennard}
{composer: George Bennard}
{year: 1913}
{key: G}
{meta: tune OLD RUGGED CROSS}
{meta: variation The hymn, sung to OLD RUGGED CROSS}
{meta: license public-domain}
{meta: tags cross}

{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
On a [G]hill far away stood an [G7]old rugged [C]cross,
The [D7]emblem of suffering and [G]shame;
And I [G]love that old cross where the [G7]dearest and [C]best
For a [D7]world of lost sinners was [G]slain.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_chorus: Refrain}
So I'll [D7]cherish the old rugged [G]cross,
Till my [C]trophies at [G]last I lay down;
I will [G]cling to the old rugged [C]cross,
And ex[G]change it some [D7]day for a [G]crown.
{end_of_chorus}

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
O that [G]old rugged cross, so de[G7]spised by the [C]world,
Has a [D7]wondrous attraction for [G]me;
For the [G]dear Lamb of God left His [G7]glory a[C]bove
To [D7]bear it to dark Calva[G]ry.
{end_of_verse}

{chorus}

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
In that [G]old rugged cross, stained with [G7]blood so di[C]vine,
A [D7]wondrous beauty I [G]see,
For 'twas [G]on that old cross Jesus [G7]suffered and [C]died,
To [D7]pardon and sancti[G]fy me.
{end_of_verse}

{chorus}

{start_of_verse: Verse 4}
To the [G]old rugged cross I will [G7]ever be [C]true;
Its [D7]shame and reproach gladly [G]bear;
Then He'll [G]call me some day to my [G7]home far a[C]way,
Where His [D7]glory forever I'll [G]share.
{end_of_verse}

{chorus}
